steps.body:
A function packages code under a name so you can run it again and again. Declare it with `function`, a name, parameters in parentheses, and a body in braces. Call it by writing its name and the arguments in parentheses.

```javascript
function double(n) {
  return n * 2;
}

console.log(double(21)); // 42
```

`return` ends the function and sends a value back to the caller, so `double(21)` *is* 42 wherever you write it. A function that never reaches a `return` gives back `undefined`, so `console.log(sayHi())` prints `undefined` after whatever `sayHi` itself prints.

Function declarations are hoisted: JavaScript sets them up before the code around them runs, so a call above the declaration still works. Declare them first anyway, because code reads better top to bottom.

**The gotcha:** keep the value on the same line as `return`. JavaScript ends the statement at a line break after `return`, so this returns `undefined`:

```javascript
function broken() {
  return
    "never seen";
}
```

